\begin_layout Standard
Here we start from the effective model described in [SUNKO] and derive the
 effective EME coupling that is presented in the main text.
 The key new insight is as follows: the effective Kondo-like coupling that
 is generated between the Pd-electrons and Cr local moments involves an
 inter-layer hybridization, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{ij}$
\end_inset

 between sites 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 If this coupling were to be affected by the fluctuations associated with
 the corresponding out-of-plane bond, 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{ij}$
\end_inset

 will be renormalized by the displacement, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 Following similar steps as in [SUNKO] we define the creation and annihilation
 operators, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}^{\left(\dagger\right)},c_{i}^{\left(\dagger\right)}$
\end_inset

and for the Pd an Cr electrons respectively.
 With these defintions, the Hamiltonian of the system is given by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
H & =H_{\text{Pd}}+H_{\text{Cr}}+H_{\text{hyb}}+H_{\text{ph,EME}},\\
H_{\text{Pd}} & =\sum_{\boldsymbol{k}\sigma}\left(\varepsilon_{\boldsymbol{k}}-\mu\right)p_{\boldsymbol{k}\sigma}^{\dagger}p_{\boldsymbol{k}\sigma},\\
H_{\text{Cr}} & =-\sum_{ij\sigma}t_{ij}^{c}c_{i}^{\dagger}c_{j}+\text{h.c}+U\sum_{i}\left(n_{i\uparrow}^{c}-\frac{1}{2}\right)\left(n_{i\downarrow}^{c}-\frac{1}{2}\right),\\
H_{\text{hyb}} & =\sum_{\left\langle ij\right\rangle \sigma}g_{ij}\left(1-\lambda\varphi_{ij}\right)\left(c_{i\sigma}^{\dagger}p_{j\sigma}+\text{h.c}\right)\\
H_{\text{ph,EME}} & =\sum_{\left\langle ij\right\rangle }\frac{\pi_{ij}^{2}}{2M}+\frac{M\tilde{\omega}_{0}}{2}\varphi_{ij}^{2}.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
here 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{ij}^{c}$
\end_inset

 is the hopping in the Cr layer, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{\boldsymbol{k}}$
\end_inset

 is the dispersion for th Pd electrons, where we keep hopping up to second
 nearest neighbours, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 determines the scale by which the hybridization is renormalized to leading
 order by the bond displacement 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{ij}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is the phonon mass, 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i\sigma}^{c}$
\end_inset

 is the density operator for Cr electrons, and 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 is the on site interaction strenght.
 In general these interactions would involve multiple orbitals in the Cr
 layer and contain the full lattice structure of PdCrO
\begin_inset Formula $_{2}$
\end_inset

, for simplicitly, however, we only consider a single orbital per site in
 the Cr layers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since it has been demonstrated that the Cr atoms are strongly correlated
 and form a Mott insulator with 120
\begin_inset Formula $^{\circ}$
\end_inset

 AFM order at low temperatrures, it is justified to take the strong coupling
 limit 
\begin_inset Formula $U\gg t^{c},g$
\end_inset

 to get a low energy effective theory in terms of local moments in teh Cr
 layer [SUNKO].
 We can obtain this low energy theory by performing a Schriffer-Wolf transformat
ion of the above Hamiltonian.
 If we only keep linear terms in the bond displacement, the resulting Hamiltonia
n will be given by :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
H & =H_{\text{Pd}}+H_{\text{lm}}+H_{\text{K}}+H_{\text{EME}}+H_{\text{ph,EME}},\\
H_{\text{lm}} & =J_{H}\sum_{\left\langle ij\right\rangle }\boldsymbol{S}_{i}\cdot\boldsymbol{S}_{j}\\
H_{\text{K}} & =\sum_{\left\langle ilj\right\rangle \sigma}\frac{4g_{ij}g_{lj}}{U}\boldsymbol{S}_{l}\cdot\left(p_{i}^{\dagger}\boldsymbol{\sigma}p_{j}\right)\\
H_{\text{EME}} & =-\sum_{\left\langle ilj\right\rangle \sigma}\frac{4g_{ij}g_{lj}}{U}\lambda\left(\varphi_{il}+\varphi_{jl}\right)\boldsymbol{S}_{l}\cdot\left(p_{i}^{\dagger}\boldsymbol{\sigma}p_{j}\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first term above is the usual Heisenberg coupling with antiferromagnetic
 exchange 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{H}=4t^{c}/U,$
\end_inset

 where we only considered nearest neighbour hopping between Cr sites.
 The second term above is the Kondo coupling that was reported in [SUNKO],
 while the third is the new EME coupling between local moments in the Cr
 layer,optical phonons and itinerant electrons.
 In what follows we consider the simplified case where adjacent Pd layers
 are aligned completely aligned and the hibridization is a constant independent
 of bond direction 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{ij}\rightarrow g$
\end_inset

.
 In this limit, we can define the Kondo and EME couplings to be 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{K}=4g^{2}/U$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\alpha}=-J_{K}\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 We then have the following Hamiltonian in momentum space
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H=H_{\text{Pd}}+H_{\text{ph}}+H_{\text{lm}}+H_{\text{int}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with the interaction given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
H_{\text{int}}= & \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{\substack{\bm{k}\bm{k}'\\
\alpha\beta
}
}J_{K}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{k}'\right)p_{\boldsymbol{k}\alpha}^{\dagger}\left[\boldsymbol{S}_{\boldsymbol{k}-\boldsymbol{k}'}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\alpha\beta}\right]p_{\boldsymbol{k}'\beta}\\
 & +\frac{1}{N}\sum_{\bm{k}\bm{k}'\bm{q}}\sum_{\ell\alpha\beta}\tilde{\alpha}_{\ell}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{k}'\right)\varphi_{\ell}\left(\boldsymbol{k}-\boldsymbol{k}'-\boldsymbol{q}\right)p_{\boldsymbol{k}\alpha}^{\dagger}\left[\boldsymbol{S}_{\boldsymbol{q}}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\alpha\beta}\right]p_{\boldsymbol{k}'\beta}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the Fourier transfomed operators are:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varphi_{\ell+j,j}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{\bm{k}}e^{-i\bm{k}\bm{R}_{j}}\varphi_{\ell}\left(\boldsymbol{k}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{i\alpha}^{\dagger}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{\bm{k}}e^{-i\bm{k}\bm{R}_{i}}p_{\boldsymbol{k}\alpha}^{\dagger}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{S}_{j}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{\bm{q}}e^{-i\bm{q}\bm{R}_{j}}\boldsymbol{S}_{\boldsymbol{q}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the momentum dependent coupligns are 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
J_{K}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{k}'\right) & =J_{K}\left[2\cos\left(k_{z}a_{z}\right)\right]\left[2\cos\left(k_{z}'a_{z}\right)\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\tilde{\alpha}_{+1}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{k}'\right) & =\tilde{\alpha}_{-1}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{k}'\right)=\tilde{\alpha}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{k}'\right)=-\lambda J_{K}\left(\boldsymbol{k},\boldsymbol{k}'\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with 
\begin_inset Formula $\bm{R}_{i}$
\end_inset

 being the position of site 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 the number of unit cells as the normalization of the Fourier transform.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the above derivation we introduced several three new parameters to the
 theory in [SUNKO], the mass of the EME mode 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

, its frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\omega}_{0}$
\end_inset

 and the EME coupling 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 As we detail in the text, these parameters only enter the theory either
 in the precise combination that corresponds to the dimensionless EME coupling,
 or in the combination 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\tilde{\omega}_{0}$
\end_inset

, leaving effectively two new parameters in the theory.
 The rest of the parameters are taken from the values provided in [SUNKO].
 Specifically, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $U=4$
\end_inset

eV and 
\begin_inset Formula $g=0.1$
\end_inset

eV which lead to a Kondo coupling of 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{K}=12$
\end_inset

 meV.
 Additionally, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{H}=6.25$
\end_inset

meV, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{nn}^{p}=568$
\end_inset

meV, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{nnn}^{p}=108$
\end_inset

meV, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=256$
\end_inset

meV.
 
\end_layout

